n1. **`SELECT *`**: It returns every column from both tables, including duplicates like `id`. Those duplicates can collide or confuse clients that read columns by name. It also pulls more data than you need and blocks index-only scans. List the columns you want.\n2. **`lower(u.email) = '...'`**: Wrapping the column in a function stops a plain index on `email` from being used, so the query probably scans the whole `users` table. You have two fixes:\n   - Add an expression index: `CREATE INDEX users_email_lower_idx ON users (lower(email));`\n   - Store emails normalized (lowercased on write) and compare `u.email = 'a@example.org'` directly. In Postgres you can use `citext` instead.\n3. **Missing index on the join column**: Make sure `orders.user_id` is indexed, or the join will scan `orders`.\n4. **No aliases or qualified columns in the select list**: This isn't a problem yet, but it becomes one once you replace `*`.\n\n**Suggested rewrite**\n\n```sql\nSELECT o.id, o.created_at, o.total,   -- whichever columns you need\n       u.id AS user_id, u.email\nFROM users u\nJOIN orders o ON o.user_id = u.id\nWHERE lower(u.email) = lower('a@example.org');\n```\n\nStarting from `users` makes the intent clearer: find one user, then fetch their orders. The planner will normally pick this order anyway. Applying `lower()` to the literal as well keeps the comparison correct if the value comes from user input.\n\nIf you tell me the database and the columns you need, I can tailor the select list and index statements. I can also edit the file for you.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}